From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bpereira <bpereira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 11:54:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Invalidate :has() on child-list changes like Gecko, not by
 parent

Any insertion in a document with a :has() rule stamped restyle_subtree() on the parent, so
adding one child of <body> recascaded the whole page. Sites that keep mounting portals into
<body> paid a full restyle for each style read in between.

Port Gecko's insertion and removal handling instead. A mutated element inherits the :has()
search direction of its parent and of its previous sibling, the elements on either side get the
side-effect pass, and the anchors that read an :empty, :nth-* or edge-child position are
invalidated through the tree-structural dependencies, from the same selector flags that already
pick which siblings to restyle. Slots, shadow hosts, dir=auto and the slow selector flags keep
the whole-parent restyle, now on removal too.
